course: dictionary
lesson: 1148
title: Briefly: a short time or a few words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Briefly» is an adverb, a word that tells us how something happens. It has two main meanings, and the context tells you which one to use.
explain: In «I briefly saw her», briefly means for a short time. The clue is saw, because seeing someone happens in a moment. In «Tell me briefly what happened», briefly means in a few words, without many details.
explain: Ask yourself: is the sentence about time, or about how much is said? Then swap in «for a short time» or «in a few words» and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Briefly means for a short time, as in «I briefly saw her».
fact: Briefly also means in a few words, without many details, as in «Tell me briefly what happened».
fact: Similar words: quickly, shortly, in short.
fact: In school, «briefly explain» means give a short answer with the main points.
fact: Useful phrases: «paused briefly», «put briefly», «presents clearly and briefly».
exercise: «I briefly saw her at the station.» What does briefly mean here? | Very slowly | *For a short time | Very loudly | In a few words
exercise: Your teacher says, «Briefly explain how plants drink water.» What should you do? | Draw a picture only | *Give a short answer with the main points | Write a long story with every detail | Whisper the answer
exercise: «Anyone knocked out, even briefly, should be checked by a doctor.» What does briefly mean here? | Forever | Very loudly | For a whole day | *For a short time
test: «Tell me briefly what happened.» Which meaning fits? | Very happily | *In a few words, without many details | Very quickly and loudly | For a long time
test: Mia says, «Each team member presents part of the argument clearly and briefly.» What should each member do? | Shout over the others | Say nothing at all | Talk for an hour about everything | *Give their part in a short, clear way
exam: «In a hamster ball, a hamster should be used only briefly, with an adult.» Which meaning of briefly fits best? | In a few words | *For a short time | Never at all | Very loudly
